SENT: There are two primary interpretations of the concept of "text": the immanent (broad, philosophically charged) and the representative (more specific). The immanent approach treats the text as an autonomous reality, focusing on the identification of its internal structure. The representative approach views the text as a specific form of presenting information about a reality external to the text itself. In linguistics, the term "text" is used in a broad sense, encompassing instances of spoken language as well. The perception of text is studied within the fields of text linguistics and psycholinguistics. For instance, I. R. Galperin defines a text as follows: "A written message, objectified in the form of a written document, consisting of a series of utterances linked by various types of lexical, grammatical, and logical connections, possessing a specific modal character and pragmatic intent, and having undergone literary refinement." | Time: 2026-11-16 14:48:41
